#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "21dc74fb-60a7-5a4f-a02b-34a300f59934" :question/math-academy-id "e-3858" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $y = 4\\sec\\left(2x-1\\right)-5$?" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form\n\n$$\ny = A\\sec(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2$. Therefore, the period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{2}=\\pi\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "15a48902-1cdc-5e6f-b363-8fda4d62e3db" :question/math-academy-id "e-3859" :question/problem "Find an expression for the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = 2\\csc\\left(x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1$." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\csc x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\sin x$, and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of\n\n$$\n2\\csc\\left(x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1\n$$\n\nwe replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nx - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} =n\\pi\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - \\frac{π}{4} &= nπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{π}{4} + nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ee6e62ca-7bc8-5c82-a852-973272e34152" :question/math-academy-id "e-3860" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $y = -2\\csc(3x+5)-1$." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\csc x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$[MATH: \\csc x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1, \\infty)]$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n$[MATH: \\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right) \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1, \\infty).]$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\leq -1, \\qquad \\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x)$, we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1$, we get\n$\\csc (3x + 5) \\le - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) \\ge 2; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\ge 2 - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\ge 1; f(x) \\ge 1$.\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1$, we get\n$\\csc (3x + 5) \\ge 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) \\le - 2; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\le - 2 - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\le - 3; f(x) \\le - 3$.\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 1, \\qquad f(x) \\leq -3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $[MATH: f(x)\\in (-\\infty, -3] \\cup [1, \\infty).]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7c633805-c725-5591-8ce1-0dfcfb28a6d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3861" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x) = 2\\csc\\left(x+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\csc x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\csc x \\leq -1\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\csc x = -1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\csc x$.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(x+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right) \\leq -1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2\\csc (x + \\frac{3π}{4}) & \\le - 2 \\\\ f(x) & \\le - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2\n$$\n\nat the local maximum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c341c33f-1a10-5989-8bb5-d46221034ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251827} {:answer/id #uuid "09ba42d6-549b-54a2-ab54-98a37557d1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251828} {:answer/id #uuid "32dd9017-ec89-5fc2-9bb4-e69437428e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251829} {:answer/id #uuid "78e762dc-68fe-5658-a702-72bfcad1cbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251830} {:answer/id #uuid "a48ae6b8-a7be-56a3-bfb6-8a460fb125a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e762dc-68fe-5658-a702-72bfcad1cbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251830} :answer-field/id #uuid "89240417-e55d-544b-9d79-ebeb5574859f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251826}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c406461-cb37-5308-b065-fa43ddd0d204" :question/math-academy-id "q-117749" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=6.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b45ea77-1cc4-53a5-a501-e24756a8a14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251834} {:answer/id #uuid "5e95e6c0-dbdd-583c-b5ef-afacbf0bf9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251835} {:answer/id #uuid "ec59f6d2-4d2b-597b-94e9-3b14c2b3f0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251836} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5866b-a8d1-5d5a-b63c-b68abdd6f18e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251837} {:answer/id #uuid "2656c6e7-a65c-52c7-9867-364405f3ae6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b45ea77-1cc4-53a5-a501-e24756a8a14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251834} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd96f271-7c0f-5536-b9a6-90c701a2cff7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251833}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "484267a9-035e-5116-a969-0b42a16d0214" :question/math-academy-id "q-117751" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7e2606c-ddcb-5e62-89e6-b3f4c76750d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251841} {:answer/id #uuid "516d6074-c6e2-5a72-b916-ceba95813a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251842} {:answer/id #uuid "1e704eba-d1bf-55cf-b525-34e9a2ad748c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251843} {:answer/id #uuid "bed8b6ee-0844-51e8-90ea-9961e7018508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251844} {:answer/id #uuid "50bbc2d4-d324-5156-af31-2ed249286017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7e2606c-ddcb-5e62-89e6-b3f4c76750d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251841} :answer-field/id #uuid "218daa9d-4ad2-5e96-9967-af00cbf5db91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251840}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2126f652-f5c8-5647-ab37-59c31ce6ad00" :question/math-academy-id "q-117752" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{6})}=12\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e991c5ea-32e8-561c-a9cb-e737aaea5588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251848} {:answer/id #uuid "dce4fc5f-576b-5ebf-bcc5-8b2094bba11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251849} {:answer/id #uuid "4e84e179-1b1d-5518-bd22-06a7c322fe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251850} {:answer/id #uuid "32cdba5c-b325-5730-aa36-a0a14ab12142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251851} {:answer/id #uuid "715a2191-122d-5c59-9c22-a4ee59b14af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "715a2191-122d-5c59-9c22-a4ee59b14af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251852} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3aed822-051a-5225-bc89-f9927490d69b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251847}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "854cbf28-abb4-5877-a950-3499f7238e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-117803" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are **local minimums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$  For now, we're interested in the local minimums (not local maximums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -3+2 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-1$ at its local maximum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84604926-e240-57c7-86d1-c86bfc6227b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251855} {:answer/id #uuid "b201738f-16b6-58df-94c9-16abac023274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251856} {:answer/id #uuid "900193c8-00b1-577f-8191-1b05b59a9a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251857} {:answer/id #uuid "42ac9ee6-4177-589d-b0b1-b9f40d904fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251858} {:answer/id #uuid "0df1546e-a2ea-55aa-ad85-dae7c47cd365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ac9ee6-4177-589d-b0b1-b9f40d904fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251858} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c0092d1-461c-55e7-82fa-af22fef96060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7364d6e7-5a70-5426-a169-e4770dee5ce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117804" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3$ at its local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and adding $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & f(x)\\ge \\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{11}{3}$ at its local minimum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4d0e8e-b30f-5398-aeaa-c00d0578b3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251862} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b1222e-3cf8-561c-a758-28433f9f5096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251863} {:answer/id #uuid "99b9a290-2983-5b27-b6a4-4e7e46e01a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251864} {:answer/id #uuid "68c2f31d-4d70-5f0c-9f5e-457acd7e177a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251865} {:answer/id #uuid "aa539e41-59db-5122-9766-161cc53ffc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99b9a290-2983-5b27-b6a4-4e7e46e01a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251864} :answer-field/id #uuid "d61a4433-272a-55d8-a18d-13c4cb48f55e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251861}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21480c51-aecb-5236-ac1a-89a3c086f12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-117805" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -4 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -4+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-3$ at the local maximum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a27ccc3-9711-56ed-a843-2604146cd5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251869} {:answer/id #uuid "f61776da-7307-5e0c-a1ed-eb917e0814fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251870} {:answer/id #uuid "1a2e4225-d5a5-5e02-a910-08bccbabee8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251871} {:answer/id #uuid "c02bced5-4d7f-5df5-a149-a3dbd06c5f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251872} {:answer/id #uuid "81521e07-f719-5253-8c43-ab5e4db54774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81521e07-f719-5253-8c43-ab5e4db54774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251873} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd32fbe0-689e-5568-9530-7e22ce3b58ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251868}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c3a3b67-0e55-5588-a98d-f584bbd72fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-117807" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)-5$ at its local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and subtracting $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\le -1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\ge 3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)-5\\ge 3-5 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)-5\\ge -2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-2$ at its local minimum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486f603c-98f8-5186-9e1a-7efb3e3ef667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251876} {:answer/id #uuid "e88541bc-44a2-5637-82be-6675bbc63d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={720}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251877} {:answer/id #uuid "473729c4-c52c-5557-900f-e662dbca0e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251878} {:answer/id #uuid "40eb3c17-66ce-592a-9acf-4534274f1631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251879} {:answer/id #uuid "78f6a5e2-83b0-5df9-ac99-19f97f077bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "486f603c-98f8-5186-9e1a-7efb3e3ef667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251876} :answer-field/id #uuid "3718ea6a-2e20-5820-a57e-24ca0c7cef5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251875}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b65d149d-e989-5d51-9258-2f05b2ccda04" :question/math-academy-id "q-117809" :question/problem "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =4\\cdot {60}^{∘}+4\\cdot n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881f85d9-fee7-5602-a809-9bc1580c5a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251883} {:answer/id #uuid "ae487f03-a07b-5905-9e44-5e4d5d8562bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251884} {:answer/id #uuid "47d44d87-5cb5-5ba6-9ed5-4975200cd061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251885} {:answer/id #uuid "27bd55e4-8ca9-558d-8509-0ef2f86c01fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251886} {:answer/id #uuid "eea69c9b-baa4-5535-a1e8-21376467bd9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eea69c9b-baa4-5535-a1e8-21376467bd9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251887} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15708cb-f416-5c1f-b9f2-986cafd6f9e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251882}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f89eb7d8-8dae-5a04-80fe-012d0776e04c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65950" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- If we add $1$ to the first interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\le -1+1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\le 0 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If we add $1$ to the second interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\ge 1+1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\ge 2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le 0,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbd4995-8a45-5508-9d61-af6378c71029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082083} {:answer/id #uuid "e27be2f7-1260-557d-aac2-ecab760fdd6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082084} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0dfe6f-a665-50d0-94a9-0997567b6e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082085} {:answer/id #uuid "343e4f72-d26e-5a25-b6be-01a13365d006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082086} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7c1bef-6ae6-5868-8e8e-26a3d6ce94cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbd4995-8a45-5508-9d61-af6378c71029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082083} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f856ef-1f01-599f-afee-ba23b0932066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082082}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0bb2a84-7705-5755-83fd-22fc24ab86c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-65962" :question/problem "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = \\csc (4x + 1) - 2$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+1)-2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+1=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =-1+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5e002b8-b792-5862-8928-eb0e8703ca91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251890} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfa3d9d-b75c-5991-a7a3-0feed12a00ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251891} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0dfb5c-81b3-573b-8634-282e0425ae60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251892} {:answer/id #uuid "0658f78a-de58-5498-9cf0-9be5a7af9712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251893} {:answer/id #uuid "d8964993-9348-5a49-a9e4-38f923395193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0658f78a-de58-5498-9cf0-9be5a7af9712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251893} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c37f1ee-433f-514c-9b79-5976e3552533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251889}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02402d59-e16a-5d97-bff4-9f897a71ac05" :question/math-academy-id "q-66027" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{3})}=6\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d217da5-3ce7-5177-ab96-d2115d1682c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082089} {:answer/id #uuid "9da6af30-8c82-502e-bc94-7e8c709d6d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082090} {:answer/id #uuid "cab1d192-b182-5540-b7d8-97a02a2b8289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082091} {:answer/id #uuid "287bb5c8-4962-53d0-99ff-f5827da0276b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082092} {:answer/id #uuid "42df44b8-b9d6-55ad-b4c8-9e5552e80daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "287bb5c8-4962-53d0-99ff-f5827da0276b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082092} :answer-field/id #uuid "83ce33d7-9cd4-5562-9f8b-bfd3f0af80b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082088}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59e0e070-bfa1-5087-9d42-f4676cd92157" :question/math-academy-id "q-66028" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y = \\frac{1}{2}\\sec (3x + \\frac{π}{4}) - 1$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=3.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48738753-ce54-567f-b5fa-51dc18d68607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251897} {:answer/id #uuid "5769d295-dfbf-5f3d-b9c8-8f68aef678e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251898} {:answer/id #uuid "099abe1e-ec73-5000-b967-b7e2043ab729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251899} {:answer/id #uuid "5879de62-023a-570f-92e0-2c64f5dbbaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251900} {:answer/id #uuid "ddca5ab2-faa2-503e-8b87-679f68d0dc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddca5ab2-faa2-503e-8b87-679f68d0dc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251901} :answer-field/id #uuid "a883533c-b52f-575d-ac56-7edfaed0bd74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251896}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2118398-ce16-51d0-88be-841a3fe7f905" :question/math-academy-id "q-66033" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and are given by the following expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfa1526e-08e2-561d-a276-087ddc72a611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251904} {:answer/id #uuid "75639471-30ca-50c4-9082-8aa60cad0486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251905} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f6db9e-f9aa-5b0a-8674-f8258c65030c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251906} {:answer/id #uuid "737900ed-76bb-5be7-878b-10d376082fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251907} {:answer/id #uuid "608489c7-f179-5df8-a8d4-c1bdc3027881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75639471-30ca-50c4-9082-8aa60cad0486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251905} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f13610-67a5-51c5-a00f-c884e113da12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dd7d3ff-dfe6-52ce-a1f9-ccab1e1260e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66060" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\ge 1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\le -1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 3,\\,f(x)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892fe9a9-ba39-54a7-b5ae-02901faff713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251911} {:answer/id #uuid "304abcfe-63c7-5b4f-bf5e-06200c7eae1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251912} {:answer/id #uuid "095f89ec-eaae-59dc-8770-23ad0507c332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} {:answer/id #uuid "4c85e617-e800-53aa-839e-7586310ae6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251914} {:answer/id #uuid "fd485259-91bf-5783-9225-4da3d8d6cedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3}]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "304abcfe-63c7-5b4f-bf5e-06200c7eae1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251912} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbef7a11-4358-51a1-917e-2386a92b5999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251910}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7579f66-27e1-5013-a0ad-1f18b27d3fbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-66063" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13657036-ac03-5d44-8f1b-28c12365374b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{5}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251918} {:answer/id #uuid "df58192c-cf88-505c-a6f1-1e8cd2251882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{4\\pi }{3}+5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251919} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cee646-2792-5356-a589-a8fcf2541b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251920} {:answer/id #uuid "927bc6bf-3bf3-564c-a7fc-0f5cb201be73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251921} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca87c84-1bc4-546a-aae1-a257330c06ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cee646-2792-5356-a589-a8fcf2541b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251920} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3396274-ed51-576a-aca2-bf352be50b21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251917}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c980f4ec-2efe-53be-925c-7d487c183465" :question/math-academy-id "q-66076" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f679244-9174-5a3e-a2aa-cad4710f87b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082107} {:answer/id #uuid "b7419de8-4b1e-532d-93ac-ba128b889541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082108} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb60a28-7108-5f06-bd37-4965851a4554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082109} {:answer/id #uuid "21e45ac5-88f9-5b09-8132-355a15c976a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082110} {:answer/id #uuid "7840d742-bada-5d6e-afa3-6258337cfa29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f679244-9174-5a3e-a2aa-cad4710f87b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082107} :answer-field/id #uuid "19555bbe-6751-5f01-8ede-b30b7bb2ca99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdecb3c9-ad89-5b16-8c08-22bbffb1f8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-66083" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}\\sec (3x + \\frac{π}{4}) + 2$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\frac{1}{2}$ and adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\le -\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}$ at the local maximum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8e760f-3a19-587b-8d46-09878bb8dc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251925} {:answer/id #uuid "06a794bb-9873-539d-898e-236ee6541407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251926} {:answer/id #uuid "2e32f494-4fdc-5906-91ec-553df3f49c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251927} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc07b5c-dca5-5f79-bee6-87954a61d92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251928} {:answer/id #uuid "65f02577-f730-50d5-9f14-098f4befc874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b8e760f-3a19-587b-8d46-09878bb8dc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251925} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a522dc-e64d-5bb9-a000-f89de898ea44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251924}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e459e49-98cd-5a2d-9766-6943742f02f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81567" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- If we add $4$ to the first interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le -1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le 3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If we add $4$ to the second interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le 3,\\,f(x)\\ge 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e20a9cf1-2dda-5971-b6e8-c2d8b4e48654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251932} {:answer/id #uuid "a76d3c5a-a8ea-5cba-bf52-b4408e2b56c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251933} {:answer/id #uuid "26a089fb-3b43-5cc9-8e3d-0ea6e14e72eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251934} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ed9330-4382-57e6-b955-2e3623d99227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251935} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fa8d91-18d1-5359-8512-96df77a26829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e20a9cf1-2dda-5971-b6e8-c2d8b4e48654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251932} :answer-field/id #uuid "11dbf014-d59a-5a90-8707-ed27ad4b9913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251931}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c8ae358-f972-53bf-a269-c0e71dace0ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-81568" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -2,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61917597-19fd-5bad-9f41-4c7f845763bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251939} {:answer/id #uuid "2566c42e-21ec-5bec-a4ef-cad60fef8105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251940} {:answer/id #uuid "c2372e9d-787c-512e-9790-894946fb3239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251941} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8be909-3d3c-55e3-9847-166d95fdca34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251942} {:answer/id #uuid "119398ef-fba1-5fb7-8287-22b0eedace3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "119398ef-fba1-5fb7-8287-22b0eedace3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251943} :answer-field/id #uuid "b26d1079-b699-5f7d-a372-1feb63b1b268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87c5b5b2-9628-59cb-9d0f-2102cae4eb6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-81581" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x+{30}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx+{30}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{30}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."}]}
